# ROLE: translator

Language spec:
# BrainCode Language Specification

**Version:** 0.1.0
**Status:** not yet bootstrapped — Sprint 0 will run automatically on the next invocation against this empty spec.

## Purpose

BrainCode is a formal syntax for expressing human agent-requests and agentic reasoning
(task decomposition) that is:

- **Expressive** — captures what natural language captures (negation, quantifiers, conditionals,
  temporal ordering, causal chains, multi-step dependency) without redundancy.
- **Broad-coverage** — generalizes to domains not seen during its own development.
- **Deterministic** — independent translators (different models, or repeated runs) converge on
  the same or equivalent expression for the same task.
- **Interpretable** — recoverable to plain-language intent by a human or LLM reader, given only
  the expression and this documentation.
- **Style-independent** — does not encode the linguistic idiosyncrasies of whichever model wrote
  it (e.g. "email my mom" vs. "email my professor" should differ in *content* the syntax
  captures — recipient, register — not in incidental phrasing).

This document is the frozen reference during any evaluation. Do not hand-edit it outside of a
sprint — use the orchestrator so `docs/changelog.md` and `docs/glossary.md` stay in sync.

## Foundations

*(To be filled in by Sprint 0 — the Searcher gathers structural notes on the inspiration
languages configured in `config.yaml -> run.bootstrap.inspiration_languages`, then the Shaper
proposes the initial basis.)*

## Constructs

*(No constructs accepted yet. Each accepted change appends or rewrites one section here: name,
grammar, semantics, one worked example, and a pointer to its `glossary.md` entry and the
sprint/changelog entry that introduced or last revised it.)*


Proposed changes available for this translation:
- [add] Lexical Primitives: grammar `identifier ::= [a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*
string     ::= '"' <chars except unescaped '"'; \" and \\n supported escapes>* '"'
number     ::= [0-9]+ ('.' [0-9]+)?
boolean    ::= "true" | "false"
comment    ::= '#' <any chars to end of line>  (ignored by parser)
reserved   ::= "task"|"if"|"then"|"else"|"and"|"or"|"not"|"for"|"each"|"in"|"any"|"all"|"true"|"false"|"let"|"end"
variable_ref ::= '$' identifier` — Defines the token-level alphabet all other constructs assume. Whitespace (spaces, tabs, newlines) is insignificant except as a statement separator; statements are separated by a newline or ';'. Blocks are delimited by explicit keyword pairs (e.g. ':' ... 'end'), never by indentation, so the grammar remains parseable without tracking column position (avoids context-sensitivity). Comments run from '#' to end of line and carry no semantic meaning. Reserved words may not be used as identifiers. Three lexical classes exist for names: a bare identifier immediately followed by '(' is a verb/task call name; an identifier prefixed with '$' is a variable reference; a bare identifier used elsewhere is an unquoted literal token (only valid where a construct enumerates allowed tokens, e.g. `true`/`false`) — free-form text must be a quoted string. This sigil/position-based rule is the sole disambiguation mechanism between task names, variables, and literals referenced later in binding-related constructs.
- [add] Action: grammar `action        ::= identifier '(' [ argument_list ] ')'
argument_list ::= argument (',' argument)*
argument      ::= identifier '=' value
value         ::= string | number | boolean | variable_ref | action` — An Action denotes a single named operation (the 'verb') executed with zero or more key=value modifier attributes, mirroring HTML's separation of element from attributes. Arguments are evaluated left to right before the action itself executes; if a value is itself a nested action, that inner action fully executes first and its result is substituted as the argument value (innermost/leftmost-first evaluation, matching ordinary function-call semantics). If the same key appears more than once in argument_list, the later occurrence overrides the earlier one (deterministic last-wins, left-to-right). Actions are effectful by default: executing one may change external state and always yields an implicit result value plus an implicit boolean status `ok` (true iff the action completed without failure). When an Action appears where a boolean is expected (see Condition), its truth value is this `ok` status unless the action's own definition names a specific boolean output field, in which case that field is used instead — this is the explicit pure/effectful disambiguation rule for the whole language.
- [add] Sequence & Binding: grammar `program    ::= statement+
statement  ::= binding | action | task_call | condition | iteration
binding    ::= 'let' identifier '=' (action | task_call)` — A program is a strictly ordered, top-to-bottom sequence of statements (flat, non-nested execution order, Python-inspired) — each statement runs to completion before the next begins; there is no implicit parallelism anywhere in BrainCode v0. `let name = ...` is the sole mechanism for passing one step's output into a later step: it binds the result of an action or task call to `name`, retrievable afterward only via `$name` (see Lexical Primitives for the sigil rule). Bindings are immutable once created; a later `let` reusing the same name shadows the prior binding for subsequent statements only, with no retroactive effect on statements already executed. A bare action or task_call statement (no `let`) executes for its effects and its result, if unbound, is discarded. Referencing a `$name` before it has been bound anywhere earlier in the same sequential scope is a static well-formedness error.
- [add] Task: grammar `task_def  ::= 'task' identifier '(' [ param_list ] ')' ':' program 'end'
param_list ::= identifier (',' identifier)*
task_call  ::= identifier '(' [ argument_list ] ')'` — A task_def introduces a named, reusable decomposition of a program (Parsel-style hierarchical decomposition): its parameters are bound as `$param` variables visible only inside its own body. Syntactically a task_call is identical to an Action call; at the call site, if a task with that identifier is in scope it is invoked (its body executed per Sequence & Binding rules using the supplied arguments), otherwise the identifier is treated as a primitive Action verb passed to the executing agent — this shared-namespace resolution is call-site-based, not declaration-order-based. A task's implicit return value is the result of its final statement; callers capture it with `let x = taskname(...)` exactly as with Action. Tasks may call themselves or one another recursively, including forming cycles; BrainCode does not itself bound recursion depth or forbid cycles, but a cyclic definition with no statically reachable base case (e.g. an unconditioned self-call) is a well-formedness warning, and an executing agent must enforce its own termination/depth limit at runtime.
- [add] Condition: grammar `condition ::= 'if' bool_expr 'then' program [ 'else' program ] 'end'
bool_expr ::= or_expr
or_expr   ::= and_expr ( 'or' and_expr )*
and_expr  ::= not_expr ( 'and' not_expr )*
not_expr  ::= [ 'not' ] atom
atom      ::= action | task_call | variable_ref | boolean | '(' bool_expr ')'` — Precedence, lowest to highest: `or`, then `and`, then `not`; `or` and `and` are left-associative binary connectives, `not` is a right-binding unary prefix on a single atom. Evaluation is strictly left-to-right and short-circuit: for `A or B`, A is evaluated first and B is evaluated (and any side effects it contains occur) only if A is false; for `A and B`, B is evaluated only if A is true. Any Action or task_call may serve as an atom — using an effectful one inside a condition executes it and its truth value is its implicit `ok` status (or named boolean output field), per the Action construct's pure/effectful rule; this is explicit so no atom is silently assumed side-effect-free. The chosen branch's program executes per Sequence & Binding; the untaken branch (including any actions nested in its condition atoms that were short-circuited away) never executes.
- [add] Iteration: grammar `iteration  ::= for_each | quantifier
for_each   ::= 'for' 'each' identifier 'in' (variable_ref | action | task_call) ':' program 'end'
quantifier ::= ('any' | 'all') '(' identifier 'in' (variable_ref | action | task_call) ',' bool_expr ')'` — The collection expression (a variable, action, or task_call yielding an ordered list) is evaluated exactly once, before iteration begins. `for each x in C: ... end` then binds `x` to successive elements of C in order and executes its program body once per element, fully sequentially — no implicit parallelism, matching the Sequence & Binding evaluation order. `x` is scoped to the loop body only. `any(x in C, cond)` and `all(x in C, cond)` are boolean quantifier atoms usable anywhere a bool_expr atom is allowed (see Condition): `any` evaluates cond over successive elements and short-circuits true on the first element where cond holds; `all` short-circuits false on the first element where cond fails. Because cond may itself contain effectful actions, only the elements visited up to the short-circuit point have their side effects executed — callers relying on quantifiers over effectful conditions must account for this partial-evaluation behavior explicitly.

