boolean    ::= "true" | "false"
comment    ::= '#' <any chars to end of line>  (ignored by parser)
reserved   ::= "task"|"if"|"then"|"else"|"and"|"or"|"not"|"for"|"each"|"in"|"any"|"all"|"true"|"false"|"let"|"end"
variable_ref ::= '$' identifier` — Defines the token-level alphabet all other constructs assume. Whitespace (spaces, tabs, newlines) is insignificant except as a statement separator; statements are separated by a newline or ';'. Blocks are delimited by explicit keyword pairs (e.g. ':' ... 'end'), never by indentation, so the grammar remains parseable without tracking column position (avoids context-sensitivity). Comments run from '#' to end of line and carry no semantic meaning. Reserved words may not be used as identifiers. Three lexical classes exist for names: a bare identifier immediately followed by '(' is a verb/task call name; an identifier prefixed with '$' is a variable reference; a bare identifier used elsewhere is an unquoted literal token (only valid where a construct enumerates allowed tokens, e.g. `true`/`false`) — free-form text must be a quoted string. This sigil/position-based rule is the sole disambiguation mechanism between task names, variables, and literals referenced later in binding-related constructs.
