{
 "foundations_overview": "BrainCode's grammar is a small, explicitly-delimited, context-free-ish core: a flat, strictly-ordered sequence of statements (no significant whitespace) built from one effectful primitive (Action), one decomposition primitive (Task), one control primitive (Condition), and one repetition primitive (Iteration), all glued together by an explicit variable-binding mechanism rather than implicit shared state. Every construct closes its own block with an explicit keyword (`end`) rather than relying on indentation, trading Python's visual economy for unambiguous, indentation-independent parseability. Modifiers attach to actions as HTML-style key=value attributes, keeping the closed-class vocabulary of verbs small while still letting any task be qualified arbitrarily. Named logical connectives (and/or/not, if/then/else, for each) are treated as a small fixed function-word class, English-inspired, with explicit precedence and short-circuit rules so no two readers can parse a compound condition two different ways.",
 "inspiration_summary": "Python inspired the flat, non-nested top-to-bottom statement sequencing and the preference for 'one obvious way to do it' in argument passing; HTML inspired separating an action's core verb from its stylistic/contextual key=value attributes; English supplied the closed-class function words (and/or/not/if/then/else/for each) that carry BrainCode's logical structure; formal-language-theory concerns motivated replacing indentation-sensitivity with explicit block-closing keywords to keep the grammar strictly context-free and deterministically parseable.",
 "summary": "Establishes a six-construct orthogonal core — lexical primitives, Action, Sequence/Binding, Task, Condition, and Iteration — covering effectful primitives, decomposition, data flow, boolean logic, and looping with explicit evaluation-order and precedence rules.",
 "changes": [
  {
   "op": "add",
   "construct_name": "Lexical Primitives",
   "grammar": "identifier ::= [a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*\nstring     ::= '\"' <chars except unescaped '\"'; \\\" and \\\\n supported escapes>* '\"'\nnumber     ::= [0-9]+ ('.' [0-9]+)?\nboolean    ::= \"true\" | \"false\"\ncomment    ::= '#' <any chars to end of line>  (ignored by parser)\nreserved   ::= \"task\"|\"if\"|\"then\"|\"else\"|\"and\"|\"or\"|\"not\"|\"for\"|\"each\"|\"in\"|\"any\"|\"all\"|\"true\"|\"false\"|\"let\"|\"end\"\nvariable_ref ::= '$' identifier",
   "semantics": "Defines the token-level alphabet all other constructs assume. Whitespace (spaces, tabs, newlines) is insignificant except as a statement separator; statements are separated by a newline or ';'. Blocks are delimited by explicit keyword pairs (e.g. ':' ... 'end'), never by indentation, so the grammar remains parseable without tracking column position (avoids context-sensitivity). Comments run from '#' to end of line and carry no semantic meaning. Reserved words may not be used as identifiers. Three lexical classes exist for names: a bare identifier immediately followed by '(' is a verb/task call name; an identifier prefixed with '$' is a variable reference; a bare identifier used elsewhere is an unquoted literal token (only valid where a construct enumerates allowed tokens, e.g. `true`/`false`) — free-form text must be a quoted string. This sigil/position-based rule is the sole disambiguation mechanism between task names, variables, and literals referenced later in binding-related constructs.",
   "worked_example": {
    "nl": "A comment noting intent, followed by a call using a string literal and a number.",
    "braincode": "# reserve a table for two\nreserve_table(party_size=2, name=\"Alex\")"
   },
   "glossary_gloss": "The token-level rules (identifiers, literals, comments, reserved words, block delimiters) that every other BrainCode construct is built from.",
   "change_type": "MAJOR",
   "rationale": "Names the lexical layer explicitly and replaces Python's indentation-sensitivity with explicit block-closing keywords, so 'strictly parseable' is a claim the grammar earns rather than assumes, per formal-language-theory guidance to avoid context-sensitive constructs.",
   "cites_source": "formal_language_notes; python_notes"
  },
  {
   "op": "add",
   "construct_name": "Action",
   "grammar": "action        ::= identifier '(' [ argument_list ] ')'\nargument_list ::= argument (',' argument)*\nargument      ::= identifier '=' value\nvalue         ::= string | number | boolean | variable_ref | action",
   "semantics": "An Action denotes a single named operation (the 'verb') executed with zero or more key=value modifier attributes, mirroring HTML's separation of element from attributes. Arguments are evaluated left to right before the action itself executes; if a value is itself a nested action, that inner action fully executes first and its result is substituted as the argument value (innermost/leftmost-first evaluation, matching ordinary function-call semantics). If the same key appears more than once in argument_list, the later occurrence overrides the earlier one (deterministic last-wins, left-to-right). Actions are effectful by default: executing one may change external state and always yields an implicit result value plus an implicit boolean status `ok` (true iff the action completed without failure). When an Action appears where a boolean is expected (see Condition), its truth value is this `ok` status unless the action's own definition names a specific boolean output field, in which case that field is used instead — this is the explicit pure/effectful disambiguation rule for the whole language.",
   "worked_example": {
    "nl": "Send the professor a formally-toned message.",
    "braincode": "send_message(recipient=\"professor\", tone=\"formal\", body=\"Requesting an extension.\")"
   },
   "glossary_gloss": "The core effectful primitive: a verb applied to key=value attribute modifiers, always producing a result and an implicit ok/failure status.",
   "change_type": "MAJOR",
   "rationale": "Decouples core semantic verbs from stylistic/contextual modifiers as HTML does with tag+attributes, letting a small closed vocabulary of verbs cover unbounded task variation without grammar bloat.",
   "cites_source": "html_notes"
  },
  {
   "op": "add",
   "construct_name": "Sequence & Binding",
   "grammar": "program    ::= statement+\nstatement  ::= binding | action | task_call | condition | iteration\nbinding    ::= 'let' identifier '=' (action | task_call)",
   "semantics": "A program is a strictly ordered, top-to-bottom sequence of statements (flat, non-nested execution order, Python-inspired) — each statement runs to completion before the next begins; there is no implicit parallelism anywhere in BrainCode v0. `let name = ...` is the sole mechanism for passing one step's output into a later step: it binds the result of an action or task call to `name`, retrievable afterward only via `$name` (see Lexical Primitives for the sigil rule). Bindings are immutable once created; a later `let` reusing the same name shadows the prior binding for subsequent statements only, with no retroactive effect on statements already executed. A bare action or task_call statement (no `let`) executes for its effects and its result, if unbound, is discarded. Referencing a `$name` before it has been bound anywhere earlier in the same sequential scope is a static well-formedness error.",
   "worked_example": {
    "nl": "Search for flights, then book the cheapest one that was found.",
    "braincode": "let results = search_flights(origin=\"NYC\", destination=\"LA\")\nbook_flight(flight=$results)"
   },
   "glossary_gloss": "Top-level flat statement ordering plus the `let`/`$name` mechanism for threading one step's output into a later step's input.",
   "change_type": "MAJOR",
   "rationale": "Establishes explicit sequential evaluation order and closes the data-flow gap (one step's output feeding a later step) with a single orthogonal binding construct rather than leaving it implicit.",
   "cites_source": "python_notes"
  },
  {
   "op": "add",
   "construct_name": "Task",
   "grammar": "task_def  ::= 'task' identifier '(' [ param_list ] ')' ':' program 'end'\nparam_list ::= identifier (',' identifier)*\ntask_call  ::= identifier '(' [ argument_list ] ')'",
   "semantics": "A task_def introduces a named, reusable decomposition of a program (Parsel-style hierarchical decomposition): its parameters are bound as `$param` variables visible only inside its own body. Syntactically a task_call is identical to an Action call; at the call site, if a task with that identifier is in scope it is invoked (its body executed per Sequence & Binding rules using the supplied arguments), otherwise the identifier is treated as a primitive Action verb passed to the executing agent — this shared-namespace resolution is call-site-based, not declaration-order-based. A task's implicit return value is the result of its final statement; callers capture it with `let x = taskname(...)` exactly as with Action. Tasks may call themselves or one another recursively, including forming cycles; BrainCode does not itself bound recursion depth or forbid cycles, but a cyclic definition with no statically reachable base case (e.g. an unconditioned self-call) is a well-formedness warning, and an executing agent must enforce its own termination/depth limit at runtime.",
   "worked_example": {
    "nl": "Define a reusable trip-planning procedure and then invoke it for LA.",
    "braincode": "task plan_trip(destination):\n  let results = search_flights(destination=$destination)\n  book_flight(flight=$results)\nend\nplan_trip(destination=\"LA\")"
   },
   "glossary_gloss": "A named, parameterized, reusable sub-program invoked like an Action; supports recursion/cycles with executor-enforced termination.",
   "change_type": "MAJOR",
   "rationale": "Gives BrainCode a hierarchical-decomposition primitive (as in Parsel) so complex requests can be broken into verifiable, reusable sub-tasks rather than one flat unstructured sequence.",
   "cites_source": "relevant_precedents:Parsel"
  },
  {
   "op": "add",
   "construct_name": "Condition",
   "grammar": "condition ::= 'if' bool_expr 'then' program [ 'else' program ] 'end'\nbool_expr ::= or_expr\nor_expr   ::= and_expr ( 'or' and_expr )*\nand_expr  ::= not_expr ( 'and' not_expr )*\nnot_expr  ::= [ 'not' ] atom\natom      ::= action | task_call | variable_ref | boolean | '(' bool_expr ')'",
   "semantics": "Precedence, lowest to highest: `or`, then `and`, then `not`; `or` and `and` are left-associative binary connectives, `not` is a right-binding unary prefix on a single atom. Evaluation is strictly left-to-right and short-circuit: for `A or B`, A is evaluated first and B is evaluated (and any side effects it contains occur) only if A is false; for `A and B`, B is evaluated only if A is true. Any Action or task_call may serve as an atom — using an effectful one inside a condition executes it and its truth value is its implicit `ok` status (or named boolean output field), per the Action construct's pure/effectful rule; this is explicit so no atom is silently assumed side-effect-free. The chosen branch's program executes per Sequence & Binding; the untaken branch (including any actions nested in its condition atoms that were short-circuited away) never executes.",
   "worked_example": {
    "nl": "If flights were found and the list isn't empty, book the cheapest; otherwise notify the user.",
    "braincode": "let results = search_flights(origin=\"NYC\", destination=\"LA\")\nif $results and not is_empty(list=$results) then\n  book_flight(flight=$results)\nelse\n  notify_user(message=\"No flights found\")\nend"
   },
   "glossary_gloss": "if/then/else branching over boolean expressions built from and/or/not with fixed precedence, left-associativity, and short-circuit evaluation.",
   "change_type": "MAJOR",
   "rationale": "Supplies English's closed-class logical function words with the explicit precedence/associativity/short-circuit rules that a bare grammar listing of and/or/not would leave ambiguous.",
   "cites_source": "english_notes"
  },
  {
   "op": "add",
   "construct_name": "Iteration",
   "grammar": "iteration  ::= for_each | quantifier\nfor_each   ::= 'for' 'each' identifier 'in' (variable_ref | action | task_call) ':' program 'end'\nquantifier ::= ('any' | 'all') '(' identifier 'in' (variable_ref | action | task_call) ',' bool_expr ')'",
   "semantics": "The collection expression (a variable, action, or task_call yielding an ordered list) is evaluated exactly once, before iteration begins. `for each x in C: ... end` then binds `x` to successive elements of C in order and executes its program body once per element, fully sequentially — no implicit parallelism, matching the Sequence & Binding evaluation order. `x` is scoped to the loop body only. `any(x in C, cond)` and `all(x in C, cond)` are boolean quantifier atoms usable anywhere a bool_expr atom is allowed (see Condition): `any` evaluates cond over successive elements and short-circuits true on the first element where cond holds; `all` short-circuits false on the first element where cond fails. Because cond may itself contain effectful actions, only the elements visited up to the short-circuit point have their side effects executed — callers relying on quantifiers over effectful conditions must account for this partial-evaluation behavior explicitly.",
   "worked_example": {
    "nl": "Book a hotel in each city on the itinerary.",
    "braincode": "for each city in $itinerary:\n  book_hotel(location=$city)\nend"
   },
   "glossary_gloss": "for-each looping over a collection, plus any/all short-circuiting boolean quantifiers, both evaluating the collection once up front.",
   "change_type": "MAJOR",
   "rationale": "Closes the iteration/collections gap explicitly (lists of stops, files, search results) rather than leaving 'for each' and 'at least one of' unaddressed, composing directly on Sequence & Binding and Condition.",
   "cites_source": "python_notes"
  }
 ],
 "_provider_used": "anthropic",
 "_model_used": "claude-sonnet-5"
}

Current language-spec.md:
# BrainCode Language Specification

**Version:** 0.1.0
**Status:** not yet bootstrapped — Sprint 0 will run automatically on the next invocation against this empty spec.

## Purpose

BrainCode is a formal syntax for expressing human agent-requests and agentic reasoning
(task decomposition) that is:

- **Expressive** — captures what natural language captures (negation, quantifiers, conditionals,
  temporal ordering, causal chains, multi-step dependency) without redundancy.
- **Broad-coverage** — generalizes to domains not seen during its own development.
- **Deterministic** — independent translators (different models, or repeated runs) converge on
  the same or equivalent expression for the same task.
- **Interpretable** — recoverable to plain-language intent by a human or LLM reader, given only
  the expression and this documentation.
- **Style-independent** — does not encode the linguistic idiosyncrasies of whichever model wrote
  it (e.g. "email my mom" vs. "email my professor" should differ in *content* the syntax
  captures — recipient, register — not in incidental phrasing).

This document is the frozen reference during any evaluation. Do not hand-edit it outside of a
sprint — use the orchestrator so `docs/changelog.md` and `docs/glossary.md` stay in sync.

## Foundations

*(To be filled in by Sprint 0 — the Searcher gathers structural notes on the inspiration
languages configured in `config.yaml -> run.bootstrap.inspiration_languages`, then the Shaper
proposes the initial basis.)*

## Constructs

*(No constructs accepted yet. Each accepted change appends or rewrites one section here: name,
grammar, semantics, one worked example, and a pointer to its `glossary.md` entry and the
sprint/changelog entry that introduced or last revised it.)*


