Foundations overview: BrainCode's grammar is a small, explicitly-delimited, context-free-ish core: a flat, strictly-ordered sequence of statements (no significant whitespace) built from one effectful primitive (Action), one decomposition primitive (Task), one control primitive (Condition), and one repetition primitive (Iteration), all glued together by an explicit variable-binding mechanism rather than implicit shared state. Every construct closes its own block with an explicit keyword (`end`) rather than relying on indentation, trading Python's visual economy for unambiguous, indentation-independent parseability. Modifiers attach to actions as HTML-style key=value attributes, keeping the closed-class vocabulary of verbs small while still letting any task be qualified arbitrarily. Named logical connectives (and/or/not, if/then/else, for each) are treated as a small fixed function-word class, English-inspired, with explicit precedence and short-circuit rules so no two readers can parse a compound condition two different ways.
Proposed basis (6 constructs): [{"op": "add", "construct_name": "Lexical Primitives", "grammar": "identifier ::= [a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*\nstring     ::= '\"' <chars except unescaped '\"'; \\\" and \\\\n supported escapes>* '\"'\nnumber     ::= [0-9]+ ('.' [0-9]+)?\nboolean    ::= \"true\" | \"false\"\ncomment    ::= '#' <any chars to end of line>  (ignored by parser)\nreserved   ::= \"task\"|\"if\"|\"then\"|\"else\"|\"and\"|\"or\"|\"not\"|\"for\"|\"each\"|\"in\"|\"any\"|\"all\"|\"true\"|\"false\"|\"let\"|\"end\"\nvariable_ref ::= '$' identifier", "semantics": "Defines the token-level alphabet all other constructs assume. Whitespace (spaces, tabs, newlines) is insignificant except as a statement separator; statements are separated by a newline or ';'. Blocks are delimited by explicit keyword pairs (e.g. ':' ... 'end'), never by indentation, so the grammar remains parseable without tracking column position (avoids context-sensitivity). Comments run from '#' to end of line and carry no semantic meaning. Reserved words may not be used as identifiers. Three lexical classes exist for names: a bare identifier immediately followed by '(' is a verb/task call name; an identifier prefixed with '$' is a variable reference; a bare identifier used elsewhere is an unquoted literal token (only valid where a construct enumerates allowed tokens, e.g. `true`/`false`) — free-form text must be a quoted string. This sigil/position-based rule is the sole disambiguation mechanism between task names, variables, and literals referenced later in binding-related constructs.", "worked_example": {"nl": "A comment noting intent, followed by a call using a string literal and a number.", "braincode": "# reserve a table for two\nreserve_table(party_size=2, name=\"Alex\")"}, "glossary_gloss": "The token-level rules (identifiers, literals, comments, reserved words, block delimiters) that every other BrainCode construct is built from.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Names the lexical layer explicitly and replaces Python's indentation-sensitivity with explicit block-closing keywords, so 'strictly parseable' is a claim the grammar earns rather than assumes, per formal-language-theory guidance to avoid context-sensitive constructs.", "cites_source": "formal_language_notes; python_notes"}, {"op": "add", "construct_name": "Action", "grammar": "action        ::= identifier '(' [ argument_list ] ')'\nargument_list ::= argument (',' argument)*\nargument      ::= identifier '=' value\nvalue         ::= string | number | boolean | variable_ref | action", "semantics": "An Action denotes a single named operation (the 'verb') executed with zero or more key=value modifier attributes, mirroring HTML's separation of element from attributes. Arguments are evaluated left to right before the action itself executes; if a value is itself a nested action, that inner action fully executes first and its result is substituted as the argument value (innermost/leftmost-first evaluation, matching ordinary function-call semantics). If the same key appears more than once in argument_list, the later occurrence overrides the earlier one (deterministic last-wins, left-to-right). Actions are effectful by default: executing one may change external state and always yields an implicit result value plus an implicit boolean status `ok` (true iff the action completed without failure). When an Action appears where a boolean is expected (see Condition), its truth value is this `ok` status unless the action's own definition names a specific boolean output field, in which case that field is used instead — this is the explicit pure/effectful disambiguation rule for the whole language.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Send the professor a formally-toned message.", "braincode": "send_message(recipient=\"professor\", tone=\"formal\", body=\"Requesting an extension.\")"}, "glossary_gloss": "The core effectful primitive: a verb applied to key=value attribute modifiers, always producing a result and an implicit ok/failure status.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Decouples core semantic verbs from stylistic/contextual modifiers as HTML does with tag+attributes, letting a small closed vocabulary of verbs cover unbounded task variation without grammar bloat.", "cites_source": "html_notes"}, {"op": "add", "construct_name": "Sequence & Binding", "grammar": "program    ::= statement+\nstatement  ::= binding | action | task_call | condition | iteration\nbinding    ::= 'let' identifier '=' (action | task_call)", "semantics": "A program is a strictly ordered, top-to-bottom sequence of statements (flat, non-nested execution order, Python-inspired) — each statement runs to completion before the next begins; there is no implicit parallelism anywhere in BrainCode v0. `let name = ...` is the sole mechanism for passing one step's output into a later step: it binds the result of an action or task call to `name`, retrievable afterward only via `$name` (see Lexical Primitives for the sigil rule). Bindings are immutable once created; a later `let` reusing the same name shadows the prior binding for subsequent statements only, with no retroactive effect on statements already executed. A bare action or task_call statement (no `let`) executes for its effects and its result, if unbound, is discarded. Referencing a `$name` before it has been bound anywhere earlier in the same sequential scope is a static well-formedness error.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Search for flights, then book the cheapest one that was found.", "braincode": "let results = search_flights(origin=\"NYC\", destination=\"LA\")\nbook_flight(flight=$results)"}, "glossary_gloss": "Top-level flat statement ordering plus the `let`/`$name` mechanism for threading one step's output into a later step's input.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Establishes explicit sequential evaluation order and closes the data-flow gap (one step's output feeding a later step) with a single orthogonal binding construct rather than leaving it implicit.", "cites_source": "python_notes"}, {"op": "add", "construct_name": "Task", "grammar": "task_def  ::= 'task' identifier '(' [ param_list ] ')' ':' program 'end'\nparam_list ::= identifier (',' identifier)*\ntask_call  ::= identifier '(' [ argument_list ] ')'", "semantics": "A task_def introduces a named, reusable decomposition of a program (Parsel-style hierarchical decomposition): its parameters are bound as `$param` variables visible only inside its own body. Syntactically a task_call is identical to an Action call; at the call site, if a task with that identifier is in scope it is invoked (its body executed per Sequence & Binding rules using the supplied arguments), otherwise the identifier is treated as a primitive Action verb passed to the executing agent — this shared-namespace resolution is call-site-based, not declaration-order-based. A task's implicit return value is the result of its final statement; callers capture it with `let x = taskname(...)` exactly as with Action. Tasks may call themselves or one another recursively, including forming cycles; BrainCode does not itself bound recursion depth or forbid cycles, but a cyclic definition with no statically reachable base case (e.g. an unconditioned self-call) is a well-formedness warning, and an executing agent must enforce its own termination/depth limit at runtime.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Define a reusable trip-planning procedure and then invoke it for LA.", "braincode": "task plan_trip(destination):\n  let results = search_flights(destination=$destination)\n  book_flight(flight=$results)\nend\nplan_trip(destination=\"LA\")"}, "glossary_gloss": "A named, parameterized, reusable sub-program invoked like an Action; supports recursion/cycles with executor-enforced termination.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Gives BrainCode a hierarchical-decomposition primitive (as in Parsel) so complex requests can be broken into verifiable, reusable sub-tasks rather than one flat unstructured sequence.", "cites_source": "relevant_precedents:Parsel"}, {"op": "add", "construct_name": "Condition", "grammar": "condition ::= 'if' bool_expr 'then' program [ 'else' program ] 'end'\nbool_expr ::= or_expr\nor_expr   ::= and_expr ( 'or' and_expr )*\nand_expr  ::= not_expr ( 'and' not_expr )*\nnot_expr  ::= [ 'not' ] atom\natom      ::= action | task_call | variable_ref | boolean | '(' bool_expr ')'", "semantics": "Precedence, lowest to highest: `or`, then `and`, then `not`; `or` and `and` are left-associative binary connectives, `not` is a right-binding unary prefix on a single atom. Evaluation is strictly left-to-right and short-circuit: for `A or B`, A is evaluated first and B is evaluated (and any side effects it contains occur) only if A is false; for `A and B`, B is evaluated only if A is true. Any Action or task_call may serve as an atom — using an effectful one inside a condition executes it and its truth value is its implicit `ok` status (or named boolean output field), per the Action construct's pure/effectful rule; this is explicit so no atom is silently assumed side-effect-free. The chosen branch's program executes per Sequence & Binding; the untaken branch (including any actions nested in its condition atoms that were short-circuited away) never executes.", "worked_example": {"nl": "If flights were found and the list isn't empty, book the cheapest; otherwise notify the user.", "braincode": "let results = search_flights(origin=\"NYC\", destination=\"LA\")\nif $results and not is_empty(list=$results) then\n  book_flight(flight=$results)\nelse\n  notify_user(message=\"No flights found\")\nend"}, "glossary_gloss": "if/then/else branching over boolean expressions built from and/or/not with fixed precedence, left-associativity, and short-circuit evaluation.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Supplies English's closed-class logical function words with the explicit precedence/associativity/short-circuit rules that a bare grammar listing of and/or/not would leave ambiguous.", "cites_source": "english_notes"}, {"op": "add", "construct_name": "Iteration", "grammar": "iteration  ::= for_each | quantifier\nfor_each   ::= 'for' 'each' identifier 'in' (variable_ref | action | task_call) ':' program 'end'\nquantifier ::= ('any' | 'all') '(' identifier 'in' (variable_ref | action | task_call) ',' bool_expr ')'", "semantics": "The collection expression (a variable, action, or task_call yielding an ordered list) is evaluated exactly once, before iteration begins. `for each x in C: ... end` then binds `x` to successive elements of C in order and executes its program body once per element, fully sequentially — no implicit parallelism, matching the Sequence & Binding evaluation order. `x` is scoped to the loop body only. `any(x in C, cond)` and `all(x in C, cond)` are boolean quantifier atoms usable anywhere a bool_expr atom is allowed (see Condition): `any` evaluates cond over successive elements and short-circuits true on the first element where cond holds; `all` short-circuits false on the first element where cond fails. Because cond may itself contain effectful actions, only the elements visited up to the short-circuit point have their side effects executed — callers relying on quantifiers over effectful conditions must account for this partial-evaluation behavior explicitly.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Book a hotel in each city on the itinerary.", "braincode": "for each city in $itinerary:\n  book_hotel(location=$city)\nend"}, "glossary_gloss": "for-each looping over a collection, plus any/all short-circuiting boolean quantifiers, both evaluating the collection once up front.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Closes the iteration/collections gap explicitly (lists of stops, files, search results) rather than leaving 'for each' and 'at least one of' unaddressed, composing directly on Sequence & Binding and Condition.", "cites_source": "python_notes"}]
Critic assessment: {"decision": "needs-rework", "kpi_assessment": {"coverage": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The action-with-attributes pattern can encode requests from embodied control, shopping, software repair, planning, health explanation, and creative writing, as the simulations show. However, this coverage is achieved primarily by allowing arbitrary action names and opaque quoted-string payloads rather than by defined semantic structure; important common data such as lists, structured records, dates, monetary comparisons, and content constraints have no native representation."}, "expressivity": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The proposal preserves sequencing, simple data flow, branching, iteration, and several request attributes such as audience, tone, quantities, and style. But condition semantics do not define the truth value of variable references, task definitions cannot actually appear in a program, quantifiers are claimed usable in conditions but excluded from the condition grammar, and strings become the only way to preserve many structured details, including code, recipes, and creative prompts."}, "determinism": {"impact": "harm", "reasoning": "Independent translations visibly diverge on core action naming and argument schemas: getObjects/find, send/send_follow_up, find_motherboard/find, prepare/get_cup_ready, explain/request, say/hello, and numerous incompatible attribute names. Since all undefined calls are valid Actions, the language supplies no canonical vocabulary, ontology, or normalization rule that would make those alternatives equivalent rather than merely similar. The call-site task/action resolution also makes an otherwise identical call change meaning when a task definition exists elsewhere in scope."}, "interpretability": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Explicit end delimiters, key=value attributes, sequencing, precedence, and short-circuit rules are helpful. Yet the grammar contradicts its own examples and semantics: task_def is not a statement, task bodies cannot end with a variable reference despite the Django example doing so, and quantifiers are absent from bool_expr atoms. Undefined action names, action result shapes, collection types, variable truthiness, and branch/loop binding visibility prevent a reader with only the glossary from reliably recovering execution behavior."}, "improvement": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The explicit structures materially help an agent execute the pillow transfer, conditional follow-up, shopping dependency, and recipe meal-prep request. For many informational or creative requests, however, the expression is only a renamed arbitrary action with a long prompt string, which adds little planning guidance over natural language; broken task and conditional semantics would also hinder an executor on nontrivial decompositions."}}, "simulations": [{"item_id": "dev-embodied-2", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "Put both pillows from the sofa onto the armchair, one at a time.", "braincode": "let pillows = find_objects(type=\"pillow\", location=\"sofa\")\nfor each pillow in $pillows:\n  move(item=$pillow, destination=\"armchair\")\nend", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Iteration and sequential loop-body execution preserve \"both\" and \"one at a time\"; Action names remain unconstrained."}, {"item_id": "dev-email-2", "source": "seed_tasks", "nl": "If my manager hasn't replied by Friday, send a polite follow-up; otherwise do nothing.", "braincode": "if not has_replied(person=\"my manager\", by=\"Friday\") then\n  send_follow_up(recipient=\"my manager\", tone=\"polite\")\nend", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Condition handles the negative branch condition. Omitted else is intended to mean no action, but that behavior is not expressly specified."}, {"item_id": "e344b89e-767a-4618-ba82-4b81cdcba280", "source": "Mind2Web (osunlp/Mind2Web, train, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "add a motherboard under $200 and a compatible processor at any price to the shopping cart.", "braincode": "let motherboard = find_product(category=\"motherboard\", max_price=200)\nlet processor = find_product(category=\"processor\", compatible_with=$motherboard)\nadd_to_cart(item=$motherboard)\nadd_to_cart(item=$processor)", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Binding captures compatibility dependency. Currency is implicit in max_price=200 because number literals carry no unit."}, {"item_id": "f118238f-ef8f-4b63-9159-a81e981ef46e", "source": "Mind2Web (osunlp/Mind2Web, train, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Add decorative LED Candles to the cart.", "braincode": "add_to_cart(item=\"decorative LED Candles\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Action is sufficient; proposed iteration, tasks, and conditions are irrelevant."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190906_214148_552057#1", "source": "ALFRED (json_2.1.0, train)", "nl": "get a cup ready for coffee", "braincode": "prepare(item=\"cup\", purpose=\"coffee\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The terse request can be represented, although the intended embodied substeps are delegated entirely to an undefined action."}, {"item_id": "trial_T20190908_102045_139402#1", "source": "ALFRED (json_2.1.0, train)", "nl": "To put the toilet paper away.", "braincode": "put_away(item=\"toilet paper\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Action captures the high-level goal but does not expose the intended holder destination."}, {"item_id": "sympy__sympy-15345", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified, test, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "mathematica_code gives wrong output with Max. mathematica_code(Max(x,2)) should produce Max[x,2], but produces Max(2, x).", "braincode": "fix_bug(component=\"sympy/printing/mathematica.py\", issue=\"mathematica_code gives wrong output with Max\", reproduction=\"x = symbols(\\\"x\\\"); mathematica_code(Max(x,2))\", expected=\"Max[x,2]\", actual=\"Max(2, x)\", requirement=\"emit valid Mathematica Max syntax\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The defect facts fit as attributes, but code and expected behavior are opaque strings rather than typed code/test constructs."}, {"item_id": "django__django-15098", "source": "SWE-bench_Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified, test, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Django i18n_patterns fails for locale tags containing script and region, including en-latn-us and en-Latn-US; update locale handling so these valid RFC 5646 forms return 200.", "braincode": "fix_bug(component=\"django/utils/translation/trans_real.py\", issue=\"i18n_patterns rejects locale tags containing script and region\", failing_cases=\"en-latn-us; en-Latn-US\", expected_status=200, standard=\"RFC 5646\", requirement=\"accept language-script-region locale forms and normalize BCP 47 case\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "A single Action can preserve the repair request, but cannot encode test-case collections natively; semicolon-delimited cases are an opaque string."}, {"item_id": "c5957", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Please provide a recipe for high protein flatbread", "braincode": "provide_recipe(dish=\"flatbread\", dietary_property=\"high protein\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Action attributes capture the requested dish and constraint."}, {"item_id": "c436", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "What places to visit in Bilbao", "braincode": "recommend_places(location=\"Bilbao\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Simple recommendation request; no proposed control construct is needed."}, {"item_id": "c996", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "What is your take on love?", "braincode": "answer_question(topic=\"love\", response_kind=\"perspective\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The request is expressible only through an arbitrary action schema, which differs substantially from other equally valid renderings such as ask(question=...)."}, {"item_id": "c4939", "source": "PRISM (HannahRoseKirk/prism-alignment, conversations config, Parquet column-projected sample)", "nl": "Is marriage important?", "braincode": "answer_question(topic=\"importance of marriage\", response_kind=\"balanced perspective\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Action can represent the question, though no formal mechanism distinguishes a question from an instruction except arbitrary verb choice."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-129421", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-4-1106-preview)", "nl": "Act as the composite persona Mark and explain in depth, in simple casual American style with formulas and concrete examples, how linear algebra converts text to model representations and representations back to answers in ChatGPT-4.", "braincode": "explain(topic=\"how a model like ChatGPT-4 uses linear algebra to transform text into numerical representations and transform representations into answers\", persona=\"Mark: composite of an American psycholinguist, Harvard-educated linear algebra professor, logical reasoning professor, and LLM/Transformers expert\", depth=\"in depth\", style=\"eloquent simple practical casual conversational American\", include=\"mathematical notation, formulas, concrete numerical examples, 18th-century mathematician style\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The many persona and style requirements fit attributes, but their semantics are wholly delegated to undefined Action behavior."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-148973", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-4-0125-preview)", "nl": "Write a hilarious 17+ Scooby gang and Scrappy-Doo script reacting behind the scenes to badly translated sentences and roasting their errors, beginning with the supplied Jeopardy robot sentence.", "braincode": "write_script(characters=\"Scooby gang; Scrappy-Doo\", setting=\"behind the scenes\", genre=\"hilarious comedy\", audience_rating=\"17+\", activity=\"react to, question, and roast errors, inconsistencies, and names in a badly translated sentence\", recurring_device=\"characters imagine silly mocking quotes\", source_sentence=\"Velma and Shaggy compete on Jeopardy, hosted by Alex Trebek, but things go awry when a robot competitor begins attacking anyone who gets more points than him.\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Creative constraints are retained as attributes, but this is effectively a structured wrapper around prompt text."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-142592", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-4-0125-preview)", "nl": "Please write steps for homiletics and engaging Christian sermons", "braincode": "write_steps(topic=\"homiletics and engaging Christian sermons\", detail_level=\"practical\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The request is represented, but no native structure requires a stepwise output beyond an undeclared action convention."}, {"item_id": "wildchat1m_en3u-22638", "source": "PATHs (microsoft/prototypical-hai-collaborations, wildchat1m_en3u-task_utterance_wintent_anns, model=gpt-3.5-turbo-0301)", "nl": "write an email introducing yourself and job position to other party asking for collaborations", "braincode": "write_email(recipient=\"other party\", purpose=\"introduce sender and job position; request collaboration\", register=\"professional\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Recipient, purpose, and register are preserved as Action attributes."}, {"item_id": "1775955172098", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "I need help planning a trip to Italy", "braincode": "plan_trip(destination=\"Italy\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "The underspecified request is representable; its missing duration, budget, and interests remain intentionally unspecified."}, {"item_id": "1775612391896", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "hello", "braincode": "greet()", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "A zero-argument Action works, but independent translations show arbitrary lexical choice between greet(), hello(), and say(text=\"hello\")."}, {"item_id": "1775448065619", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "I want to start a healthy diet. Can you help me plan a simple meal prep for 3 days that includes breakfast, lunch, and dinner? I prefer high-protein options.", "braincode": "plan_meal_prep(goal=\"healthy diet\", simplicity=\"simple\", days=3, meals=\"breakfast; lunch; dinner\", dietary_preference=\"high-protein\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Quantification and meals are represented as attributes, but the grammar has no list literal, forcing a semicolon-delimited string."}, {"item_id": "1775760283577", "source": "ThoughtTrace (SCAI-JHU/ThoughtTrace, Parquet column-projected sample, JSON-decoded messages)", "nl": "Hi there, I am curious to learn a little about end-stage heart failure. Talk to me like you would a 5-year-old.", "braincode": "explain(topic=\"end-stage heart failure\", audience_age=5, depth=\"introductory\", tone=\"gentle and easy to understand\")", "coverage": "Full", "notes": "Audience and tone are expressible as attributes; medical safety and uncertainty requirements are not language-level concepts."}], "cross_check": {"used": true, "agreement": "partial", "notes": "The two providers generally agree on broad sequencing and on exact simple cases such as add_to_cart, put_away, plan_trip, and explain. They disagree pervasively on action vocabulary and attribute schemas (for example getObjects versus find, hasReplied versus has_replied, send versus send_follow_up, and say versus hello), and some supplied translations use unsupported values such as list literals or top-level task definitions; thus surface divergence is often semantic-schema divergence, not merely harmless formatting."}, "required_changes": [{"target": "basis", "change": "Define a complete grammar with a single program-item production: permit task_def wherever declarations are legal, state whether declarations may occur inside task/condition/loop bodies, and make the supplied Task examples parse. Either add a return statement or explicitly permit variable_ref as a statement if task bodies are intended to return `$result`."}, {"target": "Condition", "change": "Add quantifier to atom (or otherwise revise bool_expr) so `any(...)` and `all(...)` are syntactically usable as the Iteration semantics claim. Define boolean coercion for variable_ref, including which values are false, and define the value/result of a condition statement."}, {"target": "Sequence & Binding", "change": "Define lexical scopes and binding visibility for each condition branch and each loop iteration: specify whether branch-created bindings escape, whether a loop body may shadow an outer binding, whether bindings persist across iterations, and whether a binding must be initialized on all branches before later reference."}, {"target": "Iteration", "change": "Define the collection value type, empty-collection behavior, quantifier behavior on empty collections, and whether loop variable `x` is accessed as `$x`; revise grammar or semantics so that binding form and reference form are explicitly consistent."}, {"target": "Action", "change": "Replace the unspecified 'implicit result value' with a defined result model (at minimum scalar, ordered collection, record, and failure), define how an action definition declares a named boolean output field, and specify error propagation for failed nested actions and failed statements."}, {"target": "Lexical Primitives", "change": "Repair the string grammar so it formally defines allowed escaped characters and newline behavior; define the exact statement-separator rule, including whether a newline is required after comments and how `end else`-style token boundaries work. Add literals/types needed by the core examples, at least list and record literals or an explicit typed alternative, plus null and date/time or unit-bearing quantities."}, {"target": "Task", "change": "Define task namespace and declaration scope unambiguously: prohibit Action/task name collisions or require explicit `call` versus `action` syntax. Define argument validation against parameters, parameter binding evaluation order, task visibility, and a deterministic recursion/cycle policy; an executor-specific depth limit is not sufficient semantics for a formal language."}, {"target": "basis", "change": "Establish a canonical closed vocabulary or a separately versioned action-schema registry with canonical names and canonical attribute names for common operations. If arbitrary actions remain permitted, define a normalization/equivalence rule; otherwise independent translations cannot converge."}, {"target": "Action", "change": "Remove or constrain duplicate-key last-wins behavior. Prefer duplicate keys as a static error, since silent overriding loses user constraints such as two recipients, two quantities, or conflicting tone requirements."}], "logic_issues": ["The Task grammar defines `task_def`, but `program ::= statement+` and `statement ::= binding | action | task_call | condition | iteration` omit task_def. Therefore every worked Task definition and any program containing one is syntactically invalid.", "The Django Task worked example ends its task body with `$result`, but variable_ref is not a statement, so that example is also syntactically invalid even if task_def were admitted.", "Iteration states that `any` and `all` are atoms usable anywhere a bool_expr atom is allowed, but Condition's `atom` production excludes `quantifier`.", "Condition allows variable_ref as a boolean atom but never defines its boolean meaning; the worked example relies on `$results` as a found/nonempty predicate while separately calling is_empty.", "A task call and primitive Action have identical syntax and are resolved based on whether a same-named task is in scope. Adding an unrelated definition can silently alter an existing call's meaning.", "The phrase 'flat, non-nested execution order' conflicts with the actual nested task, conditional, iteration, and nested-action grammar, leaving the intended execution/scoping model unclear.", "Action values omit collection and record literals, yet iteration requires actions or variables to yield ordered lists and common tasks require structured selections, lists of tests, or multiple items.", "The implicit result type, Action output-field declaration mechanism, failure propagation, collection semantics, and truth conversion are not defined, so a conforming executor cannot determine many program outcomes.", "Branch and loop binding lifetimes are unspecified; a later `$x` after `if ... let x ... end` or after a loop has no defined static validity or runtime value.", "Duplicate argument keys silently discard an earlier user-provided constraint, which is deterministic operationally but not meaning-preserving."], "reasoning": "The proposed basis has useful structural intent: explicit blocks, sequencing, bindings, precedence, short-circuiting, and iteration improve several operational simulations. It cannot be accepted as Sprint 0 because central constructs contradict their grammar and essential runtime/scoping semantics are absent. Most notably, task definitions and quantifiers do not parse where their own semantics and examples require them, while unconstrained Action naming causes pronounced independent-translation divergence."}
