Previous changes: [{"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Lexical Primitives", "grammar": "identifier    ::= [a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*\nstring        ::= '\"' stringchar* '\"'\nstringchar    ::= <any source char except '\"', '\\\\', or a raw newline> | escape\nescape        ::= '\\\\\"' | '\\\\\\\\' | '\\\\n' | '\\\\t' | '\\\\u' hexdigit hexdigit hexdigit hexdigit\nnumber        ::= '-'? digit+ ('.' digit+)?\nquantity      ::= number ws+ unit_id            # ws+ = one-or-more space/tab (not newline); e.g. `200 usd`, `3 days`\nunit_id       ::= identifier                     # not quoted; a bare unit tag\nboolean       ::= \"true\" | \"false\"\nnull_lit      ::= \"null\"\nlist_literal  ::= '[' [ value (',' value)* ] ']'\nrecord_literal::= '{' [ pair (',' pair)* ] '}'\npair          ::= identifier ':' value\nvalue         ::= string | number | quantity | boolean | null_lit | list_literal | record_literal | variable_ref | action_call | task_call\nvariable_ref  ::= '$' identifier\ncomment       ::= '#' <any chars to end of line>\nschema_pragma ::= '#!schema:' identifier '@' identifier   # required as the program's first line\nreserved      ::= \"task\"|\"if\"|\"then\"|\"else\"|\"and\"|\"or\"|\"not\"|\"for\"|\"each\"|\"in\"|\"any\"|\"all\"|\"true\"|\"false\"|\"null\"|\"let\"|\"end\"|\"act\"|\"return\"", "semantics": "Tokenization is maximal-munch: at each position the lexer consumes the longest run of identifier-characters, then checks the *entire* run against the reserved-word list — a token is a keyword only on an exact whole-run match, so `endif` lexes as one non-reserved identifier, never as `end`+`if`, and `end else` lexes as two keyword tokens because whitespace separates the runs; this is the complete boundary rule between adjacent keywords. Statements are separated by a newline or ';'; runs of consecutive separators (blank lines) collapse to one. A `#` comment consumes all characters to end of line and carries no semantic meaning, but the newline that ends it still counts as the statement separator (a comment on the final line of a file is closed by EOF instead). String literals are single-line: a raw, unescaped newline inside `\"...\"` is a lexical error; `\\n` must be used for an embedded newline. Only five escapes exist: `\\\"`, `\\\\`, `\\n`, `\\t`, `\\uXXXX`; any other backslash sequence is a lexical error. `quantity` tags a number with a bare unit identifier for money/duration/etc. (`200 usd`, `3 days`); its numeric magnitude and unit are both retrievable but this basis defines no arithmetic over quantities (future work). `null` is the sole absent-value literal. `list_literal`/`record_literal` give every construct a native way to pass ordered collections and structured data instead of ad hoc delimited strings. Three lexical classes disambiguate names: an identifier immediately followed by `(` and preceded by `act.` is a primitive-action name (governed by the schema registry, see Action); a bare identifier immediately followed by `(` with no `act.` prefix is a task-call name (governed by Task's whole-program-visible namespace); an identifier prefixed with `$` is a variable reference (see Sequence & Binding); a bare identifier anywhere else is only valid as a `unit_id` or a record-literal key. Every conforming program's first line must be a `schema_pragma`, declaring the versioned action-schema registry (name and version) that all of that program's `act.` names and argument keys are checked against — the registry's contents are intentionally not fixed by this basis (deferred, versioned artifact, analogous to HTML's separately specified tag/attribute set) but its existence and version are now a hard syntactic requirement, giving later sprints a hook to enforce canonical naming/equivalence without further core-grammar changes.", "worked_example": {"nl": "A schema-tagged program, with a comment, that reserves a table for two using a string, a number-valued deposit quantity, and a list of guest names.", "braincode": "#!schema:core@1\n# reserve a table for two with no deposit\nact.reserve_table(party_size=2, deposit=0 usd, guests=[\"Alex\", \"Sam\"])"}, "glossary_gloss": "The token-level rules (identifiers, string/number/quantity/list/record/null literals, comments, reserved words, the mandatory schema pragma, and keyword-boundary tokenization) every other BrainCode construct builds on.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Adds the missing list/record/null/quantity literal types the simulated tasks needed (shopping carts, meal-prep day counts, currency), formally defines string escapes and the comment/statement-separator and keyword-boundary rules the Critic flagged as underspecified, and introduces the mandatory schema pragma as the hook for a canonical action vocabulary.", "cites_source": "formal_language_notes; python_notes; html_notes"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Action", "grammar": "action_call   ::= 'act' '.' identifier '(' [ argument_list ] ')'\nargument_list ::= argument (',' argument)*\nargument      ::= identifier '=' value\n# no two arguments in one argument_list may share an identifier key (see semantics: static error)\nresult        ::= record { ok: boolean, value: value | null, error: record | null }", "semantics": "Every `act.<name>` call and its argument keys must be members of the schema registry version declared by the program's `schema_pragma` (see Lexical Primitives) — this basis fixes only the calling convention, not the registry's contents. Each registry entry declares a `returns` type: `scalar`, `list`, `record`, `boolean`, or `null`. Arguments are evaluated strictly left to right before the call executes; if a value is itself a nested `action_call`/`task_call`, that inner call fully executes first (innermost/leftmost-first, ordinary function-call order) and its `.value` is substituted, unless its Result had `ok=false`, in which case the outer call is never invoked and the *entire enclosing statement's* Result becomes that failed Result unchanged (its `error` propagates verbatim). A duplicate key within one `argument_list` — the same identifier appearing twice — is a static well-formedness error; the program is rejected before execution (replacing any silent last-wins behavior, since silently overriding a user-supplied constraint such as a second recipient or quantity is not meaning-preserving). Every action_call, once invoked, yields a `result` record: `ok=true` with `value` of the registry-declared `returns` type and `error=null` on success; `ok=false` with `value=null` and `error={code: string, message: string}` on failure. Boolean coercion when an action_call is used as a boolean atom (see Condition): if its registry entry declares `returns=boolean`, the atom's truth is `ok and value==true`; for every other `returns` type, the atom's truth is `ok` alone (the action still ran for effect; `value` remains retrievable via `let` but plays no role in the boolean reading). A statement-level failure that is not itself caught by a Condition checking `ok` unwinds the current block up to the nearest enclosing task boundary, whose own Result then becomes that failure (see Task); at top level an unhandled failure halts the program.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Send the professor a formally-toned message requesting an extension.", "braincode": "#!schema:core@1\nact.send_message(recipient=\"professor\", tone=\"formal\", body=\"Requesting an extension.\")"}, "glossary_gloss": "The core effectful primitive: a registry-governed verb `act.name(...)` applied to key=value attributes, always producing a typed `{ok,value,error}` Result; duplicate argument keys are a static error, not silent override.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Replaces the unspecified 'implicit result' with a concrete typed Result model, defines error propagation for nested/failed calls, makes duplicate keys a static error instead of silently discarding a user constraint, and moves primitive actions under a namespaced, schema-checked `act.` prefix to separate them from Task's identifier space.", "cites_source": "html_notes"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Sequence & Binding", "grammar": "program     ::= top_stmt*\ntop_stmt    ::= task_def | stmt\nblock       ::= stmt+\nstmt        ::= binding | action_call | task_call | condition | iteration | return_stmt\nbinding     ::= 'let' identifier '=' value\nreturn_stmt ::= 'return' value", "semantics": "A program is a strictly ordered, top-to-bottom sequence of `top_stmt`s; each statement, including all of its own nested evaluations (arguments, branch bodies, loop bodies), runs to full completion before the next top-level statement begins — this clarifies the earlier 'flat, non-nested' phrasing: execution order is strictly sequential and depth-first, while lexical nesting of blocks (task/if/for bodies) is fully permitted and evaluates by this same rule recursively. `task_def` may appear only as a `top_stmt` (top level of the program), never inside a task/condition/loop body — declarations are hoisted and whole-program visible (see Task) rather than order- or nesting-dependent, which keeps task resolution deterministic. Every block (a task body, an if-branch, an else-branch, or a for-each body) opens a fresh child lexical scope. `let name = value` binds `name` in the *current* scope only, retrievable afterward as `$name`; the binding is visible to later statements in that same scope and to any block nested later within it, but it never escapes upward past the end of its own block and is never visible in a sibling branch that wasn't taken. Because branch-local bindings simply cannot outlive their branch, there is no 'maybe bound' ambiguity to resolve across branches: referencing `$name` when no enclosing-scope `let`/param/loop-binder for `name` is active anywhere in the current scope chain is a static undefined-reference error, full stop. If a branch-computed value must be used *after* the conditional or loop that computed it, the recommended and only in-language mechanism is to factor that computation into a `task_def` ending in `return` and bind the task's Result with `let` in the outer scope (Task's `return` is the one construct that crosses a scope boundary that plain `let` cannot). A later `let` reusing a name already bound earlier in the exact same scope shadows it for subsequent statements in that scope only, with no retroactive effect on statements already executed. `return value` is legal only inside a `task_def` body (a top-level `return` is a static error); it halts that body immediately and its `value` becomes the enclosing task_call's `result.value`. A bare `action_call`/`task_call` statement executes for its effect; its Result is discarded if not captured with `let`.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Search for flights, then book the cheapest one that was found.", "braincode": "#!schema:core@1\nlet results = act.search_flights(origin=\"NYC\", destination=\"LA\")\nact.book_flight(flight=$results)"}, "glossary_gloss": "Strictly sequential, depth-first program execution; `let`/`$name` threads one step's output into a later step's input; every block opens a fresh, non-escaping child scope, so branch/loop bindings never leak.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Resolves the contradiction between 'flat, non-nested' phrasing and the actually-nested grammar, restricts `task_def` to top level so the earlier unparseable examples now parse, and gives block-local (never-escaping) scoping an explicit, self-consistent rule instead of leaving branch/loop binding lifetime undefined.", "cites_source": "python_notes"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Task", "grammar": "task_def      ::= 'task' identifier '(' [ param_list ] ')' ':' block 'end'\nparam_list    ::= identifier (',' identifier)*\ntask_call     ::= identifier '(' [ argument_list ] ')'   # identifier must not be 'act'\nargument_list ::= argument (',' argument)*\nargument      ::= identifier '=' value", "semantics": "Task names occupy a namespace entirely separate from actions: `act.<name>(...)` always denotes a registry-governed primitive; a bare `identifier(...)` always denotes a task call and must resolve against the complete set of `task_def`s declared at the top level of the program, considered as a whole (declarations are hoisted, not order-dependent) — a bare call whose identifier matches no top-level `task_def` anywhere in the program is a static undefined-reference error, never silently treated as a primitive action, so adding an unrelated `task_def` elsewhere can never change an existing call's resolved meaning (both branches of the former ambiguity are eliminated by construction). A `task_call` must supply exactly the task's declared parameters, each exactly once, by name; a missing parameter, an extra/unknown key, or a duplicate key is a static well-formedness error. Arguments are evaluated left to right in the *caller's* scope (per Action's evaluation-order rule), then bound as fresh `let`-style bindings in a new child scope belonging to the callee's body (per Sequence & Binding). The task body executes as a `block`; its Result is that of its `return value` statement if one is reached, or `{ok:true, value:null, error:null}` if the body runs to completion with no `return` reached (mirroring Condition's no-op-success rule). Recursion/cycle policy (deterministic, purely syntactic, checked statically): direct and mutual recursion between tasks are permitted, but every self- or mutually-recursive call appearing in a task's body must be lexically nested inside the `then` or `else` block of a `condition` — it may never appear as an unguarded top-level statement of the body; a task violating this is rejected as ill-formed. This syntactic check does not prove termination in general, so every executor must additionally enforce its own runtime recursion-depth limit (minimum guaranteed depth: 100) and, on breach, must yield `{ok:false, error:{code:\"RecursionLimitExceeded\", message:...}}` as that call's Result rather than crashing or looping forever.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Define a reusable trip-planning task that returns its booking results, then invoke it for LA.", "braincode": "#!schema:core@1\ntask plan_trip(destination):\n  let results = act.search_flights(destination=$destination)\n  act.book_flight(flight=$results)\n  return $results\nend\nlet la_results = plan_trip(destination=\"LA\")"}, "glossary_gloss": "A named, parameterized, top-level-only, whole-program-visible sub-program in the bare-identifier namespace (disjoint from `act.`), invoked like an action, ending optionally in `return`; recursion is allowed only when statically guarded by a Condition, plus an executor-enforced depth limit.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Fixes the unparseable worked example (task_def now legal at top level; `return $results` replaces the bare variable-ref statement that wasn't a valid statement form), eliminates call-site-dependent namespace ambiguity via the `act.`/bare-identifier split and whole-program hoisting, and replaces an executor-only depth limit with an additional static syntactic termination-shape check.", "cites_source": "relevant_precedents:Parsel"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Condition", "grammar": "condition   ::= 'if' bool_expr 'then' block [ 'else' block ] 'end'\nbool_expr   ::= or_expr\nor_expr     ::= and_expr ('or' and_expr)*\nand_expr    ::= not_expr ('and' not_expr)*\nnot_expr    ::= ['not'] atom\natom        ::= quantifier | value | '(' bool_expr ')'\nquantifier  ::= ('any'|'all') '(' identifier 'in' collection_expr ',' bool_expr ')'\ncollection_expr ::= variable_ref | action_call | task_call | list_literal", "semantics": "Precedence, lowest to highest: `or`, then `and`, then `not`; `or`/`and` are left-associative binary connectives, `not` is a right-binding unary prefix on a single atom. Evaluation is strictly left-to-right and short-circuit: for `A or B`, `A` is evaluated first and `B` (with any side effects it contains) is evaluated only if `A` is false; for `A and B`, `B` is evaluated only if `A` is true. `atom` now explicitly includes `quantifier`, `value` (which itself includes `action_call`/`task_call`/`variable_ref`/every literal), and a parenthesized sub-expression, so `any(...)`/`all(...)` are legal anywhere a boolean is expected, matching Iteration's claim. Boolean coercion (truthiness) for any atom that is not already a `boolean` literal: `null`, `false`, the number `0`, the empty string `\"\"`, the empty list `[]`, the empty record `{}`, and a `quantity` whose numeric magnitude is `0` are all false; every other literal value is true; an `action_call`/`task_call` atom's truth follows Action's ok/`returns=boolean` rule; a `variable_ref` atom's truth is the truthiness of its bound value under this same table. The chosen branch's `block` executes per Sequence & Binding; the untaken branch, including any atoms inside its own nested conditions that were short-circuited away, never executes. A condition statement's own Result is defined as the Result of the last statement executed in whichever branch was taken; if the condition is false and no `else` clause is present, no block executes and the condition statement's Result is `{ok:true, value:null, error:null}` (a defined no-op success, not an unspecified omission).", "worked_example": {"nl": "If any found flight is within budget, book the cheapest; otherwise notify the user.", "braincode": "#!schema:core@1\nlet results = act.search_flights(origin=\"NYC\", destination=\"LA\")\nif any(f in $results, act.is_within_budget(flight=$f, budget=500 usd)) then\n  act.book_flight(flight=$results)\nelse\n  act.notify_user(message=\"No affordable flights found\")\nend"}, "glossary_gloss": "if/then/[else]/end branching over boolean expressions built from and/or/not/quantifiers with fixed precedence, left-associativity, short-circuit evaluation, a full literal-truthiness table, and a defined Result even when no branch executes.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Admits `quantifier` into `atom` so Iteration's `any`/`all` are actually legal where claimed, defines a complete truthiness table (closing the `$results`-as-predicate ambiguity), and defines the condition statement's own Result including the previously-unspecified omitted-else case.", "cites_source": "english_notes"}, {"op": "revise", "construct_name": "Iteration", "grammar": "iteration       ::= for_each\nfor_each        ::= 'for' 'each' identifier 'in' collection_expr ':' block 'end'\nquantifier      ::= ('any'|'all') '(' identifier 'in' collection_expr ',' bool_expr ')'\ncollection_expr ::= variable_ref | action_call | task_call | list_literal", "semantics": "`collection_expr` is evaluated exactly once (executing any side effects of an `action_call`/`task_call` it contains) before iteration or quantification begins. The evaluated value's type must be `list` — a bare `list_literal`, a `variable_ref` bound to a list, or an `action_call`/`task_call` whose registry/return type is `list`; if the evaluated value is not list-typed, this is a runtime type error and the `for_each`/`quantifier`'s own Result is `{ok:false, error:{code:\"TypeError\", message:...}}`, with the body/predicate never executing. The bound identifier (written *without* `$` at its binding position in the grammar, exactly like a `let` name or a task parameter) is referenced *with* `$` inside the body/predicate — `for each pillow in $pillows: act.move(item=$pillow, ...) end` — consistent with the sigil rule used everywhere else in the language. `for each x in C: block end`: `block` executes fully sequentially, once per element of `C` in list order, each iteration opening a fresh child scope (per Sequence & Binding) that is discarded at the end of that iteration — `let`-bindings made inside the body do not persist across iterations, and there is no accumulator/reduce construct in v0 (a known gap; multi-step accumulation should be expressed via a recursive `task` using `return`, or is deferred as future work). An empty list makes `for_each` execute its body zero times, with Result `{ok:true, value:null, error:null}` (a defined no-op success). `any(x in C, cond)`/`all(x in C, cond)` are boolean atoms usable anywhere `bool_expr`'s `atom` permits (see Condition): `any` evaluates `cond` over successive elements in order and short-circuits true at the first element where `cond` is true, and is false over an empty list (vacuously); `all` short-circuits false at the first element where `cond` is false, and is true over an empty list (vacuously). Because `cond` may itself contain effectful `act.` calls, only the elements visited up to any short-circuit point have their side effects executed.", "worked_example": {"nl": "Put both pillows from the sofa onto the armchair, one at a time.", "braincode": "#!schema:core@1\nlet pillows = act.find_objects(type=\"pillow\", location=\"sofa\")\nfor each pillow in $pillows:\n  act.move(item=$pillow, destination=\"armchair\")\nend"}, "glossary_gloss": "for-each looping and any/all short-circuiting quantification over a once-evaluated `list`-typed collection, with defined empty-collection behavior and a required type check; the loop/quantifier variable is declared bare and referenced with `$`.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Defines the collection's required value type and the runtime error when it's absent, specifies empty-collection behavior for both `for_each` and the quantifiers (closing the previously-unspecified vacuous-truth question), and confirms the binder/reference sigil consistency the Critic asked to be made explicit rather than merely implied by example.", "cites_source": "python_notes"}]
Critic's decision: needs-rework
Critic's reasoning: This is a promising structural core: explicit sequential execution, scoped bindings, namespace separation, lists, quantities, branch precedence, and list iteration are meaningful improvements over no language. It cannot be accepted because its mandatory external action registry is absent and several central semantics—especially Result versus value binding, failure handling, whitespace/block parsing, and non-empty loop results—are internally incomplete or contradictory. These defects prevent statically valid, deterministic translations for every sampled item and force most domain content back into opaque prose strings.
Required changes (address every one explicitly): [{"target": "basis", "change": "Provide a normative minimal versioned action registry for the bootstrap schema (at least retrieval/search, select, navigate/click, move/manipulate, read/write/modify source, test, research, generate text, and present output), including each action's keys, types, effects, result type, canonical synonyms, and examples. A required but unavailable registry makes no proposed program statically checkable."}, {"target": "Sequence & Binding", "change": "Define exactly what let binds when its right-hand value is an action_call or task_call: the whole Result record or Result.value. Revise the flight, pillow, and task examples accordingly, and add explicit syntax for binding or inspecting the whole Result if it remains observable."}, {"target": "Action", "change": "Add Result field access or dedicated predicates/binders, such as $r.ok, $r.value, and $r.error, and define whether failures occurring in a condition become false, propagate, or may be explicitly handled. Remove the current claim that a Condition can check ok until such syntax exists."}, {"target": "Lexical Primitives", "change": "Define whitespace and indentation treatment globally, including whether leading spaces are ignored, and give a complete program grammar with required statement separators. The worked examples use indentation although the grammar only defines ws+ inside quantities and says the language is not indentation-sensitive."}, {"target": "Condition", "change": "Make not_expr recursively permit repeated negation (`'not' not_expr | atom`) or explicitly prohibit it in the semantics; the present grammar accepts only one not while the connective description implies a general unary operator."}, {"target": "Task", "change": "Replace the lexical guarded-recursion rule with a defined termination/resource model, or define precisely what lexically nested means for calls inside nested tasks/loops and why a condition counts as a guard. A condition whose predicate is always true still permits nontermination, while executor-specific depth behavior is not fully deterministic beyond the minimum."}, {"target": "Iteration", "change": "Define the Result of a non-empty for_each, including whether it is the final iteration's final statement Result, a list of Results, or null; currently only empty-loop behavior is specified."}, {"target": "basis", "change": "Add core structured-output and information/task primitives needed by the sampled domains: record/member projection, comparison/filter/sort/select, explicit source/citation and freshness constraints, output format/length constraints, and a source-edit/test assertion model. Do not rely on prose strings where these constraints need executor-visible meaning."}]
Logic issues: ["The schema pragma mandates registry validation, but no registry named core@1 or any registry contents are supplied. Consequently every simulated act.* call is unverifiable and may be a static error.", "Action says every call yields a Result record, while Sequence's examples use let results = act.search_flights(...) and later pass $results as a flight/list value. The language never defines implicit Result.value extraction for let.", "Action says uncaught failures propagate unless caught by a Condition checking ok, but no field projection, Result predicate, or syntax for checking ok exists; a failed action used directly as a condition atom has special coercion but cannot distinguish failure from false.", "The grammar does not define general whitespace handling or include statement separators in program, block, task_def, condition, or iteration productions, while all examples rely on newline-delimited and indented blocks.", "The grammar permits at most one not because not_expr ::= ['not'] atom, contradicting the ordinary recursive unary-connective expectation and leaving `not not true` invalid without an intentional rule.", "for_each defines its Result only for empty collections and type errors, not after one or more successful iterations or after a later iteration fails.", "The claimed static recursion guard is not a meaningful termination guarantee: recursion under `if true then` is accepted, and the lexical condition is underspecified for calls nested in loops, nested tasks, and condition predicates.", "Action's `result ::= record { ok: boolean, value: value | null, error: record | null }` is neither valid grammar under the record-literal production nor a complete definition of record/error field syntax; field access is absent.", "The task recursion policy permits depth-limit-dependent outcomes and leaves error message content executor-defined, reducing cross-executor equivalence.", "No rule specifies whether a value expression containing a failed task/action on the right side of let produces no binding, propagates through a condition, or exposes the failed Result; the stated enclosing-statement propagation does not resolve all binding cases."]
Pre-KPI assessment: {"coverage": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "Lists, records, quantities, sequencing, conditionals, loops, and task decomposition make many embodied and workflow requests structurally expressible. However, every domain-specific operation depends on an unspecified external registry, so the proposed basis alone cannot provide a usable vocabulary for web navigation, code modification, research, advice, or text generation."}, "expressivity": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The proposal preserves ordering, many values, quantities, basic branches, and iteration substantially better than an empty basis. It cannot reliably preserve important sampled constraints such as source citations, factual-currentness requirements, legal jurisdiction, exact document clauses, audience/register, uncertainty, or code-level edits without placing them inside opaque natural-language strings; it also has no field access, comparison, arithmetic, object selection, or explicit output-format mechanism."}, "determinism": {"impact": "harm", "reasoning": "Fixed precedence, scopes, separators, and namespaces improve syntactic determinism, but the absence of a supplied registry and canonical action/key definitions leaves translators free to invent incompatible verbs such as act.search_web, act.answer_question, act.generate_text, and act.modify_file. The unclear distinction between a call's Result and its value further makes equivalent dataflow translations unlikely."}, "interpretability": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "The explicit schemas, lexical rules, scope rules, and short-circuit semantics are a useful start. Interpretability is undermined by undefined registry behavior, an unspecified whitespace/block-token treatment despite indentation in examples, inconsistent Result/value semantics, and an impossible claimed ability to inspect ok when no member-access or Result-binding syntax exists."}, "improvement": {"impact": "mixed", "reasoning": "For simple embodied sequences and list iteration, explicit ordered calls would help a weaker executor. For most sampled advisory, writing, web, and software tasks, invented opaque action names and prose instructions in strings provide little actionable decomposition beyond the original request, while missing error inspection and result selection prevent reliable plans."}}
