ywords (`then`/`else`/`:` ... `end`), never by indentation; the worked examples below indent purely for human readability, and an unindented or inconsistently-indented rewrite of any example in this spec parses identically. `NEWLINE` and `;` are the only two token kinds that ever act as statement separators; one or more of them in a row (`SEP`, e.g. a blank line) collapses to a single separator boundary (see Sequence & Binding's `program`/`block` grammar, which is built from `SEP`). Tokenization is maximal-munch: at each position the lexer consumes the longest run of identifier-characters, then matches the *whole* run against `reserved`; `endif` is one non-reserved identifier, never `end`+`if`. A `#` comment consumes to end of line and carries no semantic meaning, but the newline that closes it still counts as a `SEP` (a final-line comment is closed by EOF). String literals are single-line — a raw newline inside `\"...\"` is a lexical error, `\\n` must be used for an embedded newline — and only the five listed escapes exist; any other backslash sequence is a lexical error. `null` is the sole absent-value literal. Four lexical classes disambiguate bare-looking names: (1) the fixed two-token sequence `act` immediately followed by `.` always opens a registry-governed primitive-action name (see Action / Action Registry core@1); (2) any other identifier immediately followed by `(` with no `act.` prefix is a task-call name (see Task); (3) `$identifier` is a variable reference; (4) an identifier immediately followed by `.` that is *not* the reserved word `act` opens a `postfix_expr` field projection (see Projection, Comparison & Try) — this is distinguished from a `number`'s decimal point because a `.` inside `number` is always immediately surrounded by digits on both sides, never by identifier characters. Every conforming program's first line must be a `schema_pragma` naming the versioned action-schema registry (`name@version`) that all `act.` names, argument keys, and their types are checked against; this basis now ships `core@1` as that registry's normative bootstrap contents (see Action Registry core@1).", "worked_example": {
