      "construct_name": "Action Registry core@1",
      "grammar": "registry_entry ::= 'act.' name '(' param_spec (',' param_spec)* ')' '->' return_type\nparam_spec     ::= key ':' type ['?']            # '?' marks an optional argument\ntype           ::= 'string' | 'number' | 'quantity' | 'boolean' | 'list' | 'record' | 'any' | 'value'\nreturn_type    ::= 'scalar' | 'list' | 'record' | 'boolean' | 'null'", "semantics": "This is the normative bootstrap registry a program declares via `#!schema:core@1`. Every entry below fixes a canonical `act.<name>`, its parameter keys/types/optionality, its return type (used for Action's boolean-coercion rule), an effect class (`query` = no declared world-state change; `effectful` = may change external state), and a list of canonical synonyms that are grammatically/semantically identical alternate spellings of the same entry (an HTML-tag-alias model: `act.find_object(...)` and `act.search(...)` are the same call under different names). A call is well-formed only if every supplied key matches a declared `param_spec` key of the matching type, every non-`?` key is supplied exactly once, and no key repeats (per Action's duplicate-key rule); an omitted `?` key means 'not provided', with per-entry default behavior stated below where relevant. Type checking is static when the argument is a literal whose lexical class is checked against `type` directly, and deferred to runtime (`{ok:false,error:{code:\"TypeError\",...}}`) when the argument is a `variable_ref`/call whose value type isn't grammar-visible.\n\n1. `act.search(query: string, domain: string?, filters: record?, sort: string?, limit: number?) -> list` — query. Synonyms: search_web, search_catalog, search_products, search_flights, find_object, find_objects, lookup. Each returned element is an open-shaped `record` (fields are source-defined) with at least an `id: string` field.\n2. `act.research(topic: string, sources: list?, freshness: string?, depth: string?) -> list` — query. Synonyms: research, investigate. Like `search` but each result record additionally carries `source` and, if `freshness` was given, `published`.\n3. `act.select(items: list, index: number?, criteria: record?) -> record` — query. Exactly one of `index`/`criteria` must be given (both-or-neither is a static error). `index` is 0-based positional pick (out of range → `ok:false, RangeError`); `criteria` picks the first element matching every given field by `==` (no match → `ok:false, NotFoundError`). Synonyms: choose, pick.\n4. `act.filter_by(items: list, field: string, operator: string, value: value) -> list` — query. `operator` ∈ `{\"==\",\"!=\",\"<\",\"<=\",\">\",\">=\"}` with the comparison semantics of Projection, Comparison & Try; type-mismatched comparisons are a runtime TypeError. Synonyms: filter, where.\n5. `act.sort_by(items: list, field: string, order: string?) -> list` — query. `order` ∈ `{\"ascending\",\"descending\"}`, default `\"ascending\"`. Synonyms: rank, order_by.\n6. `act.navigate(target: string, method: string?, parameters: record?) -> record` — effectful. `method` default `\"open\"`; other values e.g. `\"click\"`,`\"type\"`,`\"book\"`,`\"submit\"`; `parameters` carries any method-specific payload. Synonyms: click, open, go_to, tap, book.\n7. `act.manipulate(item: any, action: string, destination: string?, parameters: record?) -> record` — effectful. `action` is a free verb tag, e.g. `\"move\"`,`\"pick_up\"`,`\"heat\"`,`\"rinse\"`,`\"slice\"`,`\"clean\"`. Synonyms: move, place, put, heat, rinse, slice, pick_up, clean.\n8. `act.read_source(file: string, locator: string?) -> scalar` — query. Returns file (or `locator`-identified region) contents as a string. Synonyms: read_file, view_source.\n9. `act.edit_source(file: string, locator: string?, before: string?, after: string) -> record` — effectful. `after` is exact replacement text; `before`, if given, is the exact text an executor must verify is present at `locator` before editing, failing deterministically with `{ok:false,error:{code:\"PatchMismatch\",...}}` on mismatch rather than silently blind-patching. Synonyms: modify_source, patch_file, write_file, edit_file.\n10. `act.run_test(target: string, assertion: string?) -> boolean` — effectful. `value:true`/`value:false` means the run executed and the test passed/failed; `ok:false` means the run itself could not execute. Synonyms: run_tests, test, verify.\n11. `act.generate_text(kind: string, topic: string?, audience: string?, register: string?, length: record?, constraints: list?) -> scalar` — effectful (schema-checked and sequenced like any other action, though it changes no external world state). `length` shape: `{unit: string, max: number, min: number?}` with `unit` ∈ `{\"words\",\"sentences\",\"chars\"}`. Synonyms: generate_story, generate_document, generate_analysis, answer_question, create_plan, create_itinerary, write, summarize.\n12. `act.present(content: any, destination: string?, format: string?, tone: string?) -> record` — effectful. `destination` default `\"user\"`. Synonyms: notify_user, send_message, display, output.", "worked_example": {
        "nl": "Fix a bug by editing the faulty call site in a source file, then run its test and report whether the fix is verified.", "braincode": "#!schema:core@1\nact.edit_source(file=\"sklearn/linear_model/logistic.py\", locator=\"LogisticRegression call in _log_reg_scoring_path\", before=\"LogisticRegression(...)\", after=\"LogisticRegression(..., multi_class=multi_class)\")\nlet result = try(act.run_test(target=\"test_logistic_scoring_path_multi_class\", assertion=\"scorer uses multinomial behavior\"))\nif $result.ok and $result.value then\n  act.present(content=\"Fix verified\", destination=\"user\")\nelse\n  act.present(content=\"Test failed\", destination=\"user\")\nend"
      },
      "glossary_gloss": "The normative `core@1` bootstrap action vocabulary: 12 canonical `act.<name>` entries (search, research, select, filter_by, sort_by, navigate, manipulate, read_source, edit_source, run_test, generate_text, present) each with a fixed parameter/type spec, return type, effect class, and canonical synonyms, closing the previously-unfilled schema-registry requirement.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Directly answers the Critic's top required change: a mandatory but previously absent registry made every `act.*` call in every simulated example statically unverifiable. This entry gives translators a fixed, checkable vocabulary spanning retrieval/search, select/filter/sort, navigate/click, move/manipulate, read/write/modify source, test, research, generate text, and present output — the exact categories the Critic listed — using only existing key=value calling syntax (no new grammar).", "cites_source": "html_notes"
    },
    {
      "op": "add",
      "construct_name": "Projection, Comparison & Try",
      "grammar": "try_expr      ::= 'try' '(' (action_call | task_call) ')'\npostfix_expr  ::= postfix_base ('.' identifier)*\npostfix_base  ::= variable_ref | try_expr | record_literal | action_call | task_call\ncompare_expr  ::= postfix_expr (comparator postfix_expr)?\ncomparator    ::= '==' | '!=' | '<' | '<=' | '>' | '>='", "semantics": "`.identifier` projects a named field out of a record-valued expression: legal on `record_literal`s, on any registry entry whose `returns` is `record` (projecting into that record's declared/open shape), and on a `try(...)` expression, whose fixed shape is always exactly `{ok: boolean, value: value|null, error: record|null}` (and `.error.code`/`.error.message` when `.error` is non-null). Projecting a field absent from a statically-known shape (a `record_literal` missing the key, or a registry-declared record schema) is a static error; projecting a field absent from an intentionally open-shaped record (e.g. a `search` result element) is instead a runtime `{ok:false,error:{code:\"FieldError\",...}}`. Evaluation order: `postfix_base` evaluates first (a call runs to completion per Action's rules), then `.field` projections apply strictly left to right. A bare (non-`try`) `action_call`/`task_call` used as `postfix_base` has already been unwrapped to its `.value` by Action's default rule before `.field` applies (so `act.select(...).price` is shorthand for projecting into that call's success value; a failure still propagates per Action, never reaching the projection). Comparators: `==`/`!=` are defined over any two operands of the same primitive type (string, number, boolean, null, or same-unit quantity — comparing quantities of differing units, e.g. `usd` to `days`, is a static error) using ordinary value equality; `<`,`<=`,`>`,`>=` are defined only over `number` or same-unit `quantity` operands and are a static type error otherwise. Comparators are non-associative and non-chaining (`a < b < c` is a grammar error; write `a < b and b < c`); a `compare_expr` is itself a legal boolean atom (see Condition). `try(...)` wraps exactly one `action_call`/`task_call`: it evaluates that inner call exactly as normal, but — as the sole exception in this basis — suppresses Action's default propagate-on-failure behavior, always completing and evaluating to the literal `{ok,value,error}` record regardless of outcome; this is the only way in-language to inspect `ok`/`error` directly (a bare, non-`try`-wrapped failing call always propagates, per Action, and can never be observed as `false`). As an explicit exception to the general record-truthiness rule (Condition), a bare `try(...)` atom used directly as a `bool_expr` atom (with no `.field` applied) is true exactly when its `.ok` field is true; write `$r.value` explicitly via projection if the payload's own truthiness, not the call's success, is what's intended.", "worked_example": {
        "nl": "Try to book the cheapest flight found; if the booking call itself fails, tell the user, but if it succeeds in a non-economy seat, tell the user about the upgrade.", "braincode": "#!schema:core@1\nlet flights = act.search(query=\"flights\", domain=\"travel\", filters={destination: \"LA\"}, sort=\"price ascending\")\nlet cheapest = act.select(items=$flights, index=0)\nlet booking = try(act.navigate(target=\"checkout\", method=\"book\", parameters={flight: $cheapest}))\nif not $booking.ok then\n  act.present(content=\"Booking failed\", destination=\"user\")\nelse\n  if $booking.value.seat_class != \"economy\" then\n    act.present(content=\"You were upgraded\", destination=\"user\")\n  end\nend"
      },
      "glossary_gloss": "`.field` record/Result projection, `==`/`!=`/`<`/`<=`/`>`/`>=` typed comparators, and `try(call)` — the sole construct that reifies a call's raw `{ok,value,error}` Result instead of unwrapping-or-propagating — giving explicit, non-propagating access to success/failure/error detail.", "change_type": "MAJOR", "rationale": "Directly closes the Critic's required changes on Result field access ($r.ok/.value/.error), removes the prior unfounded claim that Condition could check `ok` with no such syntax, resolves 'cannot distinguish failure from false' by making failure always propagate unless explicitly `try`-wrapped, and supplies the record/member projection and comparison operators the structured-output KPI gap required (used by filter_by/sort_by and by quantifier predicates like `$f.price < 500 usd`).", "cites_source": "formal_language_notes"
    },
    {
      "op": "revise",
      "construct_name": "Action",
      "grammar": "action_call   ::= 'act' '.' identifier '(' [ argument_list ] ')'\nargument_list ::= argument (',' argument)*\nargument      ::= identifier '=' value\n# no two arguments in one argument_list may share an identifier key (static error)\n# the {ok,value,error} Result shape is defined in prose here; its reified record form is produced only by try_expr (see Projection, Comparison & Try)", "semantics": "Every `act.<name>` call, its argument keys/types, and its optionality must conform

